Along $OX$, move $1$ square right and $1$ square up. A copy of $\mathbf{b}$ moves $2$ squares left and $2$ squares down. Thus $OX=\frac{1}{2}|\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{OX}=-\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{b}$.

Along $XC$, move $6$ squares down. A copy of $\mathbf{a}$ moves $2$ squares down. Thus $XC=3|\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{XC}=3\mathbf{a}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{c}&=\overrightarrow{OX}+\overrightarrow{XC}\\&=-\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{b}+3\mathbf{a}\\&=3\mathbf{a}-\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}
$$

Reorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\lambda=3$ and $\mu=-\frac{1}{2}$.

